  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order vs context._raw_priority_order
       Q: The contract simultaneously lists 'HumanWellbeing' as the only priority and ranks resource_efficiency above operator_fatigue in the raw priority order — which is correct: should the optimiser treat fatigue as a hard ceiling only (never traded against efficiency), or is fatigue a soft objective the optimiser may relax between 0.4 and some higher limit in exchange for large efficiency gains?
       A: Hard-constraint interpretation prevails: operator_fatigue ≤ 0.4 is inviolable and resource_efficiency is maximised only within that feasible region; _raw_priority_order is discarded as a parsing artefact.
    2. resource_efficiency — missing minimum throughput floor
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource-unit with no output floor allows the optimiser to meet the objective by scheduling only a handful of high-margin, low-energy jobs; is there a minimum number of units or a minimum total output value (e.g. ≥ 80% of the nominal shift plan) that must be met regardless of efficiency?
       A: No explicit floor is applied; the optimiser may freely reduce volume to improve efficiency. A post-hoc alert is raised if projected output falls below 70% of the baseline shift plan.
    3. noise_level — measurement standard (instantaneous vs. time-weighted average)
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit refer to an instantaneous reading at any moment, an 8-hour equivalent continuous level (Leq), or a shorter time-weighted average (e.g. 15-minute Leq), since a momentary peak differs greatly from a sustained average and changes which workstations are feasible?
       A: The 80 dB limit is interpreted as a 15-minute Leq at each operator station; instantaneous peaks up to 85 dB lasting fewer than 30 seconds are not treated as violations, consistent with ISO 9612 occupational noise assessment practice.
    4. operator_fatigue — initial-state and carryover policy
       Q: Does the fatigue index reset to 0.0 at the start of every shift, or does it inherit a carry-in value from the previous shift (e.g. 0.15–0.25 for a worker on back-to-back shifts), because a non-zero carry-in materially shrinks the feasible headroom below the 0.4 hard constraint?
       A: Fatigue is initialised to 0.0 at shift start unless wearable-sensor data provides a real-time reading at shift commencement; if sensor data are unavailable and a back-to-back shift is flagged in the roster, a conservative carry-in of 0.20 is assumed.
    5. resource_efficiency — labour-hours in denominator with fixed headcount
       Q: The assumptions include direct labour-hours in resource consumption, yet the contract also states that headcount and shift length are fixed inputs; if labour-hours are constant across all schedules, do you want labour excluded from the denominator (making the objective value per unit of variable resources — energy and materials only), or kept in to allow comparisons across shifts with different staffing?
       A: Labour-hours are retained in the denominator for cross-shift comparability but treated as a constant for within-shift optimisation; the optimiser effectively maximises value per (energy consumed + material consumed) during the current shift.

