  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 30% demand increase has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric worker-protection constraints and cannot be accepted as-is. The Fatigue Index reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (overage: +0.2001, representing a 50% breach of the limit), and operational noise reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (overage: +4.9 dB, a 6.1% breach). Both constraints exist to protect floor personnel from occupational health risks — cumulative musculoskeletal fatigue and noise-induced hearing impairment respectively. The CBPA governance layer has blocked this schedule pending human-in-the-loop authorisation. A remediation decision is required before production can resume at the elevated demand level.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Preferred): Add a Second Production Shift: Schedule a second operating shift to absorb the 30% volume increase across a larger workforce. Each individual shift retains a workload profile that keeps FatigueIndex ≤ 0.40 and noise exposure ≤ 80.0 dB. Short-term staffing actions required: activate on-call labour pool, adjust maintenance windows, and re-sequence changeovers between shifts. This is the only option that fully honours all human-centric constraints with no limit relaxation. Expect an 8% deadline gap during the 3–5 day ramp-up period.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Relax Noise Ceiling with Mandatory PPE and Micro-Break Protocol: Raise the operational noise ceiling from 80.0 dB to 85.0 dB (the NIOSH 8-hour permissible exposure level) with mandatory class-A hearing protection for all floor personnel and enforced hourly rotation between high-noise and low-noise stations. Concurrently, introduce a mandatory 10-minute micro-break every 90 minutes to reduce the FatigueIndex from 0.6001 toward 0.40; modelling indicates breaks alone close approximately 60% of the fatigue gap. A 5% residual deadline gap remains while break scheduling is embedded. This option partially relaxes one constraint under compensatory controls but does not guarantee full fatigue compliance.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Temporary Dual-Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Oversight: Authorise a time-boxed 14-day window in which the FatigueIndex ceiling is raised to 0.65 and the noise ceiling is raised to 85.0 dB. Mandatory compensatory controls must be active for the entire window: class-A hearing protection, a minimum 1-in-4 station rotation, daily fatigue self-assessments logged in the MES, one mandatory recovery day per worker per week, and a physician-led health review at day 14 before any extension. This option closes the deadline gap immediately but places workers above baseline safety thresholds and carries occupational health and regulatory risk. Managerial sign-off and documented worker consent are required prior to activation.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
